    <!-- The Tradeoff Explanation -->
    <div class="reveal max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-10 sm:mb-16 bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-5 sm:p-8">
      <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-slate-900 mb-4">The @loadable/component Tradeoff</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">
        You <em>can</em> SSR every loadable component &mdash; but the architecture forces a painful choice:
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <div class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-6 h-6 bg-amber-200 text-amber-800 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold">1</span>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800">SSR&rsquo;ing everything delays TTFB and FCP</strong> &mdash; <code>@loadable</code>&rsquo;s ChunkExtractor requires <code>renderToString</code>, which cannot stream. The server must finish rendering all components before sending any HTML.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-6 h-6 bg-amber-200 text-amber-800 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold">2</span>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800">Every SSR&rsquo;d component still needs JS for hydration</strong> &mdash; the HTML is visible but frozen until its JavaScript downloads, parses, and executes. More components = more JS = higher TTI.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-6 h-6 bg-amber-200 text-amber-800 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold">3</span>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600"><strong class="text-slate-800">So teams are forced to choose:</strong> SSR critical components for fast FCP (non-critical content missing from HTML), or SSR everything for complete HTML (but slow FCP and heavy hydration).</p>
